{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on his own terms not as an inductive inference of future truth, but as a methodological rule for maximizing the severity of future tests; accepting this costs us the ability to claim the preferred theory is 'more likely true,' restricting the justification solely to its superior utility as a target for falsification.","counterconditions":["If one assumes that 'acting on' a theory logically entails believing it will succeed in the future, the distinction collapses into induction.","If Popper's own personal admissions of preferring theories for practical safety imply a hidden inductive belief, the methodological defense is merely rhetorical.","If the concept of 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) is required to justify the preference, and verisimilitude itself relies on inductive assumptions about the structure of the world, the argument fails."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The mechanism distinguishes between 'acting on' a theory (selecting it as the current best basis for application and further testing because it has withstood the most severe attempts at refutation) and 'believing in' a theory (assigning it higher logical probability). The preference is a pragmatic commitment to the theory that offers the most information content and has survived the hardest trials, thereby providing the highest potential for learning if it is eventually falsified. This avoids induction by treating corroboration as a historical report of test severity, not a predictor of future performance.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85}],"simulation_proposals":[]}